title: Check configured address identity against the node key
doc:
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    `--listen-addr` and `--public-addr` now accept the identity components an operator is likely
    to paste back from an advertised or published address: a trailing `/p2p/<peer id>` on any
    address, and a `/certhash/<hash>` before it on a `webrtc-direct` address.

    Both are checked against the node key and stripped before use. The node refuses to start if
    either disagrees with the key, and the error names the address, the component given, the one
    the node key yields, and where that key comes from (`--node-key`, the node key file, or a key
    generated on this start). An identity component anywhere else in the address, or a repeated
    one, is rejected as malformed.

    Previously a `/certhash` in these flags was always rejected, and a `/p2p` was handed to the
    network backend unchecked.
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    `NetworkConfiguration::validate_and_complete_webrtc_addresses` is renamed to
    `validate_and_complete_addresses`. Besides completing the public `webrtc-direct` addresses
    with the node's `/certhash`, it now checks the `/p2p` and `/certhash` components of every
    listen and public address against the node key and strips them, on both network backends.
    Running it twice is idempotent. When an address carries an identity, the node key is resolved
    and pinned into `NetworkConfiguration::node_key` even without a WebRTC listener.

    `sc_network::error::Error` gains two variants: `MismatchedAddressIdentity` and
    `MalformedAddressIdentity`.
crates:
- name: sc-cli
  bump: minor
- name: sc-network
  bump: major
